Cross-File Generic Type Aliases
================================

A generic type alias declared in one file of a multi-file module
must be usable with full generic syntax (`Name[T]`) from any
other file in the same module — same rule as generic functions
and generic structs.

Per reference §Modules + §Generic Type Aliases.

Pins the regression fixed at sysl@2c4f1c095 ("generic type
aliases visible across files of same module"):

  - `type Name[T] = ...` declared in `generic_helpers.lsysl`
    resolves with `Name[int]` syntax from this sibling file.
  - Resolves in function-parameter type position.
  - Resolves at multiple concrete instantiations side-by-side.
  - Two-parameter aliases work cross-file (`type Name[A, B]`).
  - Newtype-shaped alias (body is the type variable itself)
    works.

What this file does NOT pin:

- Same-file generic alias surfaces (covered by
  `generic_alias_basic.lsysl` in the parent directory).
- Cross-MODULE generic alias import (would need an explicit
  `import` and a different module — this file pins the
  cross-FILE / same-module path).
- Alias-as-struct-field-type (the field-type analyzer gap
  documented at the bottom of `generic_alias_basic.lsysl`
  is separate from the sibling-visibility regression pinned
  here; it would block this test the same way it blocks the
  in-file test).

File-local prefix `Gacf` (Generic-Alias Cross-File).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features.generics.siblings


Function-typed alias — single type parameter
---------------------------------------------

The helper file declares `type GhUnary[T] = (T) -> T` and
`gh_apply_unary(f: GhUnary[int], x: int) -> int = f(x)`.

### Pass a closure literal matching the imported alias' instantiation

The call site supplies a closure whose shape `int -> int` is
exactly what `GhUnary[int]` denotes. If the cross-file alias
import is broken, the helper function itself fails to compile
(the alias name doesn't resolve in the parameter type) and the
whole unit fails to build.

    #test
    test_gacf_unary_closure_arrow() -> unit
        val r = gh_apply_unary(x -> x + 1, 41)
        assert(r == 42, s"42; got $r")

### Pass the bare-call placeholder `_ * _` at the same alias

`_ * 2` is a single-arg closure inferred to `(int) -> int` and
must match `GhUnary[int]` the same way the explicit `x -> ...`
form does.

    #test
    test_gacf_unary_placeholder() -> unit
        val r = gh_apply_unary(_ * 2, 21)
        assert(r == 42, s"42; got $r")

### Same alias, second call site — distinct test

Pin that a second `gh_apply_unary` call with a different
closure body resolves the same way as the first. We keep this
in its own test rather than chaining both calls in one body
because SVM has a known stack/closure regression with two
back-to-back HOF calls in a single function body (see ROADMAP
note on `apply_twice` in `generic_fn_basic.lsysl`; this file
inherits the constraint).

    #test
    test_gacf_unary_second_call_site() -> unit
        val b = gh_apply_unary(x -> x * 5, 4)
        assert(b == 20, s"b=$b")


Function-typed alias — two type parameters
-------------------------------------------

`gh_apply_bin(f: GhBin[int, int], a: int, b: int) -> int` uses
the two-parameter alias `type GhBin[A, B] = (A, A) -> B`.

### Pass a two-arg closure matching `GhBin[int, int]`

    #test
    test_gacf_bin_two_args() -> unit
        val r = gh_apply_bin(_ + _, 10, 20)
        assert(r == 30, s"10+20; got $r")

### Two-arg closure with body that uses both params

    #test
    test_gacf_bin_two_args_arrow() -> unit
        val r = gh_apply_bin((a, b) -> a * b + 1, 6, 7)
        assert(r == 43, s"6*7+1; got $r")


Newtype-shaped alias — body is the type variable
-------------------------------------------------

The helper declares `type GhIdAlias[T] = T`. There is no
codegen for this kind of alias — it's pure type-level
abbreviation. The test pins that the sibling file can name
`GhIdAlias[int]` in a function signature without the analyzer
rejecting it as "not a generic type" (the exact symptom from
the regression).

    gacf_use_id_alias(x: GhIdAlias[int]) -> int = x + 100

### Newtype alias instantiated as a parameter type

    #test
    test_gacf_id_alias_int_param() -> unit
        val r = gacf_use_id_alias(7)
        assert(r == 107, s"got $r")

### Newtype alias at a different concrete instantiation

    gacf_use_id_alias_str(s: GhIdAlias[string]) -> int = len(s)

    #test
    test_gacf_id_alias_string_param() -> unit
        val r = gacf_use_id_alias_str("hello")
        assert(r == 5, s"got $r")


Alias in a locally-declared function-parameter type
---------------------------------------------------

Beyond using the helper's `gh_apply_unary`, declare a brand-new
local function in **this** file whose parameter type names the
sibling-imported alias `GhUnary`. This pins the analyzer
resolving the imported alias *during analysis of this file's
declarations*, not just the helper's.

    gacf_local_apply(f: GhUnary[int], x: int) -> int = f(x) + 1

### Local function using sibling-imported alias resolves and runs

    #test
    test_gacf_local_uses_imported_alias() -> unit
        val r = gacf_local_apply(x -> x * 3, 4)
        assert(r == 13, s"4*3+1; got $r")


Two distinct sibling-imported aliases coexist
----------------------------------------------

Both aliases must resolve from the helper file. We pin each in
its own one-call test rather than mixing them in a single body
because of the same SVM HOF-chaining stack regression noted
above. The compile-time fact this section pins — that both
`GhUnary` and `GhBin` resolve as types in this file — is
already exercised by the standalone tests above; the runtime
companion is two isolated calls.

### Single `GhUnary[int]` call site — sanity

    #test
    test_gacf_unary_alone() -> unit
        val u = gh_apply_unary(x -> x + 10, 5)
        assert(u == 15, s"unary; got $u")

### Single `GhBin[int, int]` call site — sanity

    #test
    test_gacf_bin_alone() -> unit
        val b = gh_apply_bin(_ - _, 100, 15)
        assert(b == 85, s"bin; got $b")
